  "estimatedMinutes": 2,
  "statement": "A paper triangle with sides of lengths \\(3,\\) \\(4,\\) and \\(5\\) inches, as shown, is folded so that point \\(A\\) falls on point \\(B.\\) What is the length in inches of the crease?",
  "answerChoices": [
    {
      "label": "A",
      "text": "\\(1+\\dfrac{1}{2} \\sqrt{2}\\)"
    },
    {
      "label": "B",
      "text": "\\(\\sqrt{3}\\)"
    },
    {
      "label": "C",
      "text": "\\(\\dfrac{7}{4}\\)"
    },
    {
      "label": "D",
      "text": "\\(\\dfrac{15}{8}\\)"
    },
    {
      "label": "E",
      "text": "\\(2\\)"
    }
  ],
  "answer": {
    "letter": "d",
    "value": "15/8"
  },
  "solutions": [
    {
      "id": "sol.amc-10a-2018-p13.live",
      "kind": "proof-sketch",
      "text": "The crease is the perpendicular bisector of \\(\\overline{AB}.\\) Let \\(\\overline{DE}\\) be the crease.\n\nBy \\(AA\\) similarity, \\(\\triangle ADE\\sim\\triangle ACB.\\) Therefore, \\[\\dfrac{BC}{AC}=\\dfrac{DE}{AD}.\\] Plugging in the side lengths gives \\[\\dfrac34=\\dfrac{DE}{\\frac{5}{2}},\\] so \\(DE=\\dfrac{15}{8}.\\)\n\nThus, D is the correct answer.",
      "hints": [
        "The crease is the perpendicular bisector of \\(AB\\)",
        "Use similarity with the original \\(3\\)-\\(4\\)-\\(5\\) triangle"
      ],
